diepxuan (5.8.6+ppa~1) noble; urgency=medium

  * fix(service): stop leaking Thread objects in ServiceScheduler. Every task
    run created a `threading.Thread` and registered it in
    `ServiceContext.threads` without ever removing it. With `vm_sync` at 10s
    and `route_monitor` at 5s that is ~25,900 Thread objects/day; after 11
    days a host held ~290k objects ≈ 700 MB RSS (measured 2.5 KB/Thread).
    Threads are now unregistered in a `finally` block and run as daemons.
  * fix(service): add a re-entrancy guard. If a task's previous run is still
    alive, the tick is skipped. Prevents pile-up when a task takes longer
    than its interval (e.g. vm_sync DNS retry ~20s vs interval 10s).
  * fix(service): `ServiceContext.join_all()` now clears both tracking
    structures after joining, so shutdown does not retain references.
  * fix(logging): remove duplicated log lines. The systemd unit redirected
    stdout to /var/log/ductnd/ductnd.log while Python's RotatingFileHandler
    wrote the same file, so every line was emitted twice (56 "Scheduler
    started" lines for ~28 real starts). The unit now sets
    `Environment=RUNNING_UNDER_SYSTEMD=1` and lets journald collect stdout;
    Python skips its own file handler in that mode.
  * fix(systemd): move `StartLimitIntervalSec` / `StartLimitBurst` from
    `[Service]` to `[Unit]`. systemd ignored them in the wrong section
    ("Unknown key 'StartLimitIntervalSec' in section [Service]").
  * fix(host): `_host_domain()` now prefers the `search` directive in
    /etc/resolv.conf, falling back to parsing `socket.getfqdn()` and then
    `DEFAULT_DOMAIN`. The fallback rejects IP-shaped suffixes: a Proxmox
    host whose /etc/hosts contains `postgresql.192.168.1.1` used to yield
    the "domain" `192.168.1.1`, a zone that does not exist.
  * fix(vm): `vm:sync` no longer exits silently when the DNS API answers
    HTTP 200 with `{"status":"error"}`. The case is logged as an error with
    domain, zone and message. Previously the command returned 0 with no
    output and no record was ever published.

